course: dictionary
lesson: 3815
title: Forgery: a fake that tricks people
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A forgery is a copy made to trick people into thinking it is real. Look at the clues around the word. «Forgery means signing someone else's name, or faking a paper, to make people believe it is real.» The words fool and believe show that a forgery is meant to deceive.
explain: «The Louvre lets registered artists copy paintings in its galleries, with rules about size so the copies cannot pass as the originals; selling a copy as the real thing is forgery, a crime.» The rules tell you that copying is allowed, but pretending a copy is the original is the trick that makes it forgery.
explain: Test your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «Signing someone else's name to fool people is called forgery, and it is a serious kind of lie.» If you put "a fake made to trick people" in place of forgery, the sentence still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
fact: A forgery is a copy of something such as a painting, a banknote, a document or a signature.
fact: The purpose of a forgery is to trick people into thinking it is real.
fact: Forgery is also the crime of making such false copies.
fact: «A clever forgery» means a fake that is very good at fooling people.
fact: «Forging a note turns one problem into two: the thing in the note and the forgery.»
fact: Copying a parent's signature on a note is forgery, and it is dishonest.
exercise: What is a forgery? | *A copy made to trick people into thinking it is real | A coin that someone has spent | A brand new painting made by a famous artist | A lost document in a library
exercise: «Copying a parent's signature on a note is forgery.» Why is it forgery? | It is a note that says thank you | *It is a copy that tries to fool people into thinking a grown-up signed it | It is a kind message to a teacher
exercise: Which word means the crime of making false copies? | Painting | *Forgery | Banknote | Signature
test: «The Louvre lets registered artists copy paintings, but selling a copy as the real thing is forgery.» What is the crime here? | Painting in a gallery | Following the size rules | *Selling a copy as the real thing
test: Why is signing someone else's name called forgery? | It is a clever way to write a letter | *It is a lie, because the paper says a grown-up agreed when they didn't | It is a kind of lie, because the paper says a grown-up agreed when they didn't | It is a painting that hangs in a gallery
exam: What is the best meaning of «forgery»? | *A fake copy made to trick people into thinking it is real | A painting that hangs in a museum | A rule about the size of paintings | A kind message written by a friend
